.\" tcpflow(1). Every option of src/cli.rs must be described here
.\" (tests/manpage.rs checks it). View with: man ./doc/tcpflow.1
.TH TCPFLOW 1 "2026-11-08" "tcpflow 2.0.0-dev" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tcpflow \- TCP flow recorder
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tcpflow
.RB [ \-aBcCDgIJKlpqsvVZ0 ]
.RB [ \-b
.IR max_bytes ]
.RB [ \-d
.IR level ]
.RB [ \-e | \-x | \-E
.IR name ]
.RB [ \-f
.IR max_fds ]
.RB [ \-F
.IR flags ]
.RB [ \-i
.IR iface ]
.RB [ \-L
.IR name ]
.RB [ \-m
.IR bytes ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR outdir ]
.RB [ \-r
.IR file ]...
.RB [ \-R
.IR file ]...
.RB [ \-S
.IR name = value ]...
.RB [ \-T
.IR template ]
.RB [ \-U
.IR user [: group ]]
.RB [ \-w
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-X
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-z
.IR dir ]
.RI [ expression ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tcpflow
captures data transmitted as part of TCP connections (flows) and stores
it in a way that is convenient for protocol analysis and debugging.
Rather than showing packet by packet information,
.B tcpflow
reconstructs the actual data streams and stores each flow in its own
file.
It understands TCP sequence numbers and reconstructs the streams
correctly regardless of retransmissions or out-of-order delivery.
A typical connection gives two files, one for each direction.
.PP
.B tcpflow
reads packets from capture files
.RB ( \-r ,
.BR \-R ,
.BR \-l )
or, without them, captures live from a network interface
.RB ( \-i ).
Capture files may be pcap or pcapng, and may be compressed with gzip
.RI ( .gz ),
bzip2
.RI ( .bz2 ),
xz or lzma
.RI ( .xz ,
.IR .lzma )
or zip
.RI ( .zip ,
the first entry); they are decompressed by
.B tcpflow
itself.
.PP
The optional
.I expression
is a libpcap filter (see
.BR pcap\-filter (7)),
such as
.BR "'tcp port 80'" .
All remaining arguments are joined with spaces, so the expression may be
given as one quoted argument or as several.
Only packets that match it are processed.
.PP
Supported link layers are Ethernet (with VLAN tags), Linux cooked
capture, raw IP, BSD loopback (NULL and LOOP), PPP, Token Ring,
802.11 (plain, radiotap and Prism).
IPv4 and IPv6 are supported; IP fragments are not reassembled.
.PP
When a flow is closed (FIN, RST,
.B tcp_timeout
or the end of input),
.B tcpflow
runs the post-processors
.RB ( \-a ,
.BR \-e )
on its file and adds the flow to the DFXML report.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Input
.TP
.BI \-r " file"
Read packets from a capture file.
May be repeated; the files are read in order.
.B \-
is standard input.
.TP
.BI \-R " file"
Read a capture file only to finish flows that are already open: no new
flows are created.
The
.B \-R
files are read after all
.B \-r
files.
This is meant for a series of files captured over time: process file
.I n
with
.BI \-r " file(n)"
and file
.I n+1
with
.BI \-R " file(n+1)" .
May be repeated.
.TP
.B \-l
Treat the remaining arguments as capture files, as if each had a
.B \-r
in front, instead of a filter expression.
For example
.B tcpflow \-o out \-l *.pcap
reads all pcap files in the current directory.
.TP
.BI \-i " iface"
Capture live on the network interface
.IR iface .
Without
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-r ,
.B \-R
or
.BR \-l ,
.B tcpflow
captures on the first interface libpcap reports.
.B \-i
together with input files is an error.
Live capture runs until SIGINT, SIGTERM or SIGHUP; then the open flows
are closed and the report is written.
.TP
.B \-p
Do not put the interface into promiscuous mode.
It may already be in promiscuous mode for another reason.
.SS Output files
.TP
.BI \-o " outdir"
Write flow files and the report into
.IR outdir ,
default the current directory.
The directory is created (one level) if it does not exist.
.TP
.BI \-T " template"
Name flow files with
.I template
(see
.BR "FILE NAMES" ).
If the template has no
.BR %c ,
.B %C%c
is appended.
.TP
.BI \-F " flags"
File name and output flags, applied in the order given; several may be
combined, as in
.BR \-Fct .
Because
.B \-T
replaces the whole template, flags given before it are lost.
.RS
.TP
.B c
Always append the connection counter: the first file gets
.BR c0 .
.TP
.B t
Prepend the flow start time in Unix seconds and
.BR T .
.TP
.B T
Prepend the flow start time in ISO 8601.
.TP
.B k
Put flow files into directories of 1000 flows
.RB ( %K/ ).
.TP
.B m
Two directory levels, for up to a million flows.
.TP
.B g
Three directory levels, for up to a billion flows.
.TP
.B X
Store no flow files; the report is still written.
Cannot be combined with post-processors.
.TP
.B M
Add the MD5 of every flow to the report (the
.B md5
post-processor).
.RE
.TP
.BI \-b " max_bytes"
Store at most
.I max_bytes
bytes of each flow; the rest is discarded.
The default is no limit.
.TP
.BI \-m " bytes"
A sequence number gap of more than
.I bytes
starts a new flow file (with the next connection counter).
The default is 16777216 (16 MiB).
.TP
.BI \-f " max_fds"
Keep at most
.I max_fds
flow files open at once.
When the limit is reached, the least recently written file is closed and
reopened later.
The default is the process file limit minus 6; with
.B \-I
it is halved.
.TP
.BI \-X " file"
Write the DFXML report to
.I file
instead of
.IR outdir/report.xml .
.TP
.B \-I
Write a packet index
.I F.findx
next to each flow file
.IR F .
It has one line per stored segment:
.RS
.IP
.IB offset | seconds . microseconds | length
.RE
.IP
where
.I offset
is the position in the flow file and
.I length
the number of bytes stored.
The lines are sorted by offset when the flow is closed.
.TP
.B \-K
Write one pcap file per flow instead of the reassembled data.
The name is the flow file name plus
.BR .pcap ;
flows whose names are the same share a file.
No post-processors, commands or alerts run with
.BR \-K .
.TP
.BI \-w " file"
Write the IP packets that were not processed as TCP (UDP, ICMP,
fragments, malformed packets, ignored SYNs) to
.I file
in pcap format.
.TP
.B \-q
Do not warn when the output directory ends up with 10000 files or more
(see
.BR \-Fk ).
.SS Console output
.TP
.B \-c
Print the data of each segment to standard output, in arrival order,
instead of writing files.
Each segment is preceded by the flow name and
.BR ": " .
No report is written.
.TP
.B \-C
Like
.BR \-c ,
without the flow name.
Unlike
.BR \-c ,
the report is still written.
.TP
.B \-s
Replace bytes that are not printable ASCII, newline or carriage return
with
.B .
when printing.
This is the default.
.TP
.B \-B
Print the bytes as they are.
.TP
.B \-D
Print a hex dump, 32 bytes per line.
Implies
.BR \-B .
.TP
.B \-0
Do not print a newline after each segment.
.TP
.B \-g
Colour each segment by direction: blue for client to server, red for
server to client.
.TP
.B \-J
Print each segment as a JSON line with
.BR src_host ,
.BR src_port ,
.BR dst_host ,
.B dst_port
and
.B payload
(the bytes as numbers).
Implies
.B \-c
unless
.B \-C
is given.
.TP
.BI \-L " name"
Take the POSIX named semaphore
.I name
around each printed segment, so that several
.B tcpflow
processes printing to the same terminal or pipe do not mix their output.
The semaphore is created with value 1 if it does not exist.
.SS Post-processing
.TP
.B \-a
Run all post-processors: the same as
.BR "\-e all" .
.TP
.BI \-e " name"
Enable a post-processor:
.RS
.TP
.B http
Extract the body of each HTTP response in a server-to-client flow into
.IB flowfile \-HTTPBODY\- nnn . ext\fR,
where
.I nnn
is the response number from 001 and
.I ext
comes from the
.BR Content\-Type .
Chunked encoding is removed, and gzip and deflate bodies are
decompressed unless
.BR \-Z .
A body is written up to
.B http_max_body
bytes.
.TP
.B md5
Add the MD5 of every flow file to the report.
.TP
.B all
Both.
.RE
.IP
.BR netviz ,
.B tcpdemux
and
.B wifiviz
are accepted for old command lines and do nothing.
.TP
.BI \-x " name"
Disable a post-processor.
.TP
.BI \-E " name"
Disable all post-processors, then enable
.IR name .
.TP
.B \-Z
Do not decompress gzip or deflate HTTP bodies.
.TP
.BI \-S " name" = value
Set a parameter (see
.BR PARAMETERS ).
May be repeated.
.SS Privileges
.TP
.BI \-U " spec\fR, " \-\-relinquish\-privileges= spec
Drop root privileges once the first input or the capture device is open.
.I spec
is
.IR user ,
.IR uid ,
.IR user : group ,
.IR uid : gid
or
.RI : group .
A user gives its primary group unless a group is named; a numeric uid
without a passwd entry needs a group.
Before dropping, the output directory, the report and the
.B \-w
file are handed to the new user if
.B tcpflow
created them.
Inputs opened later must be readable by the new user.
Any failure is fatal.
.TP
.BI \-z " dir\fR, " \-\-chroot= dir
Change the root directory to
.I dir
when dropping privileges.
Needs
.B \-U
with a user other than root.
The output directory
.B \-o
.I O
is
.IR dir/O .
.SS Other
.TP
.BI \-d " level"
Debug level for messages on standard error: 0 is silent, 1 (the default)
prints minimal status, 10 is verbose, higher levels are for developers.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
The same as
.BR "\-d 10" .
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the options and exit;
.B \-hh
also prints the parameters and file name codes.
.SH PARAMETERS
Set with
.BI \-S " name" = value\fR.
An unknown name is a warning.
.TP
.BI enable_report= 0
Do not write the report.
.TP
.BI tdelta= N
Add
.I N
seconds to every packet timestamp.
.TP
.BI tcp_timeout= N
Close flows that have been idle for more than
.I N
seconds of capture time.
.TP
.BI tcp_cmd= CMD
After a flow is closed, run
.I CMD
with the flow file as its last argument.
The command runs as
.BR "sh \-c 'CMD \(dq$1\(dq' tcpflow" " " \fIPATH\fR,
so the file name is never parsed by the shell.
At most 10 commands run at a time, and
.B tcpflow
waits for all of them before it exits.
.TP
.BI http_cmd= CMD
The same for each HTTP body file.
.TP
.BI tcp_alert_fd= N
Write a line
.BI open\(rs t path
when a flow file is created and
.BI close\(rs t path
when its flow is closed to the open file descriptor
.IR N .
.TP
.BI http_alert_fd= N
The same for HTTP body files.
.TP
.BI http_max_body= N
The largest HTTP body file in bytes; 0 means no limit.
The default is 1073741824 (1 GiB).
.TP
.BI packet\-buffer\-timeout= N
The libpcap read timeout for live capture in milliseconds, default 10.
.SH FILE NAMES
The default template is
.BR %A.%a\-%B.%b%V%v%C%c ,
for example
.PP
.RS
.B 192.168.001.103.36322\-171.025.221.158.00080
.RE
.PP
for data sent from 192.168.1.103 port 36322 to 171.25.221.158 port 80.
A new file is never overwritten: while the name exists, the connection
counter is increased, so a second run into the same directory gives
.B ...c1
files.
The codes are:
.TP
.BR %A ", " %B
Source, destination address: four zero-padded octets for IPv4
.RB ( 010.000.000.001 ),
the usual text form for IPv6.
.TP
.BR %a ", " %b
Source, destination port, 5 digits.
.TP
.BR %E ", " %e
Source, destination MAC address; zeros if not Ethernet.
.TP
.BR %V ", " %v
.B \-\-
and the VLAN ID, if the flow has one.
.TP
.BR %T ", " %t
Flow start time: ISO 8601 UTC
.RB ( 2017\-11\-17T22:57:10Z ),
Unix seconds.
.TP
.BR %c ", " %C ", " %#
The connection counter if it is above 0;
.B c
if it is above 0; the counter always.
.TP
.BR %N ", " %K ", " %M ", " %G
The flow number in groups of 3 digits: units, thousands, millions,
billions.
.TP
.B %S
The session ID, 20 digits; both directions of a connection share it.
.TP
.B %%
A literal
.BR % .
.PP
A
.B /
in the expanded name creates directories, for example
.PP
.RS
.B tcpflow \-r packets.pcap \-o out \-T %A/%a/%B/%b/%c%N.flow
.RE
.SH REPORT
Unless
.B \-c
or
.B \-S enable_report=0
is given,
.B tcpflow
writes a DFXML report,
.I outdir/report.xml
by default
.RB ( \-X ).
It records the command line, the system, a summary and one
.B <fileobject>
per closed flow, with its file name, size and a
.B <tcpflow>
element with the attributes
.BR startime ,
.BR endtime ,
.BR family ,
.BR src_ipn ,
.BR dst_ipn ,
.BR srcport ,
.BR dstport ,
.BR packets ,
.BR len ,
.B caplen
and, if present,
.BR mac_saddr ,
.BR mac_daddr ,
.B out_of_order_count
and
.BR violations .
With
.B md5
it adds a
.BR <hashdigest> ,
with
.B http
a
.B <byte_runs>
entry for each body file.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success.
1 for invalid options, an input or device that cannot be opened, an
invalid filter, a failure to drop privileges, or a read error in an
input file (the other files are still processed).
.SH SECURITY
Files in the output directory are never opened through symbolic links,
and new flow files are created exclusively.
Commands from
.B tcp_cmd
and
.B http_cmd
get the file name as an argument, never as shell text.
When capturing as root, use
.B \-U
to drop privileges after the device is open.
.SH EXAMPLES
Store the flows of a capture file in
.IR out ,
with HTTP bodies and MD5 digests:
.PP
.RS
.B tcpflow \-a \-o out \-r capture.pcap
.RE
.PP
Only port 80, from a compressed file:
.PP
.RS
.B tcpflow \-o out \-r capture.pcap.gz 'port 80'
.RE
.PP
Print live traffic to the terminal:
.PP
.RS
.B tcpflow \-i eth0 \-c
.RE
.PP
Capture live as root, then run as nobody, with flows binned 1000 to a
directory:
.PP
.RS
.B tcpflow \-i eth0 \-U nobody \-o out \-Fk
.RE
.PP
Traffic between helios and hot or ace, with a report in
.IR flows.xml :
.PP
.RS
.B tcpflow \-X flows.xml \-o out host helios and \e( hot or ace \e)
.RE
.PP
One pcap file per flow:
.PP
.RS
.B tcpflow \-K \-o out \-r capture.pcap
.RE
.SH BUGS
IP fragments are not reassembled: flows with fragmented packets are not
stored correctly.
.PP
Report bugs at
.IR https://github.com/simsong/tcpflow/issues .
.SH AUTHORS
Originally written by Jeremy Elson; maintained by Simson L. Garfinkel.
tcpflow 2.0 is a rewrite in Rust by mirdukkkkk.
.SH SEE ALSO
